assembly/ - the runtime in assembly
-----------------------------------

    start_aarch64.S   Program entry point for Linux on aarch64.  The
                      kernel enters _start with argc, argv and envp on
                      the stack; the file clears the frame chain, calls
                      main(argc, argv, envp) and hands main's return
                      value to stdoc_sys_exit.  There are no
                      constructors, no ".init_array" walk and no atexit
                      handling: the freestanding startup path does not
                      provide them.  Documented in docs/io.
    start_x86_64.S    The same for Linux on x86_64.  It also aligns
                      the stack to 16 bytes before the call, as the
                      System V AMD64 ABI requires.
    syscall_aarch64.S The system-call trampoline for aarch64.  It
                      converts the ordinary C calling convention into
                      the Linux system-call convention (number in x8,
                      arguments in x0..x5, result in x0) with a single
                      "svc #0".  Documented in docs/io.
    syscall_x86_64.S  The system-call trampoline for x86_64: the
                      number goes in rax, the fourth argument in r10,
                      and the kernel is entered with "syscall".
                      Documented in docs/io.


io/ - the input/output layer
----------------------------

    The sources here are ISO C++23.  Every function the headers declare
    is defined with C linkage, so the archive exports the C interface
    and nothing else; the declarations live in include/stdoc_io.hxx.

    file.cxx          The three standard streams (stdoc_stdin,
                      stdoc_stdout, stdoc_stderr) and the staging
                      logic: stdoc_stream_write, stdoc_stream_flush
                      and stdoc_stream_discard.  The stream objects are
                      "constinit", so they need no dynamic
                      initialization.  Documented in docs/io.
    vfprintf.cxx      The formatting engine, stdoc_vfprintf.  Parser
                      and formatter live in one translation unit.
                      Documented in docs/formatting.
    printf.cxx        stdoc_printf: the stdoc_stdout wrapper around
                      stdoc_vfprintf.
    fprintf.cxx       stdoc_fprintf: the same for a given stream.
    syscall_linux.cxx The operating-system wrappers
                      (stdoc_sys_write, stdoc_sys_read, stdoc_sys_close,
                      stdoc_sys_exit) and the only place where Linux
                      system-call numbers appear.  Documented in
                      docs/io.
    string.cxx        The internal helpers stdoc_strlen, stdoc_memcpy
                      and stdoc_memset, which exist so that the
                      library links with "-nostdlib" without taking
                      symbols from a host C library.


mem/ - dynamic memory
---------------------

    heap.cxx          The allocator: a static arena, first-fit block
                      allocation with splitting and merging, the safe
                      variants that terminate the process on a
                      detected fault, and the accounting the tests
                      assert on.  The arena is an array of "std::byte"
                      and the block state is a fixed-width scoped
                      enumeration, so the on-arena layout is the same
                      on every target.  Documented in docs/heap.


include/ - the headers
-----------------------

    libstdoc.h        The C header: the streams, stdoc_printf,
                      stdoc_fprintf and the dynamic memory functions
                      stdoc_malloc, stdoc_free, stdoc_malloc_safe and
                      stdoc_free_safe.  The comments in this file are
                      the contract of the library.  It depends only on
                      "<stdarg.h>" and "<stddef.h>", so it works in a
                      freestanding build.  Documented in
                      docs/formatting and docs/heap.
    libstdoc.hpp      The C++ entry point: it includes libstdoc.h
                      inside an "extern "C"" specification so the
                      symbols are not mangled.  No C++ runtime is
                      required.
    stdoc_io.hxx      The internal interface shared by the I/O layer
                      and the platform layer: the stream structure,
                      STDOC_BUFFER_SIZE and the internal function
                      declarations.  It is not installed, and it is
                      kept ISO C17 so the C tests can include it; the
                      declarations are wrapped in an "extern "C"" for
                      the C++23 implementation.
    stdoc_mem.hxx     The internal header of the allocator:
                      STDOC_HEAP_SIZE and the accounting queries used
                      by the tests.  It is not installed and follows
                      the same rules as stdoc_io.hxx.


